proof.text:
The two composites $a_Wb_W$ and $r_Wc_W$ are equal, since the two inclusions $K\to L'$ agree. Put $e_W=a_Wb_W=r_Wc_W$ and let $\operatorname{Ex}_e$ be the exchange for this outer field-extension square. Write $C_{u,v}=c^D_{u,v}:v^{-1}u^{-1}\xrightarrow{\sim}(uv)^{-1}$ for the canonical composition isomorphism. Clause (1) of [lem-kun-bc-base-change], applied first to $K\subseteq K'\subseteq L'$ and then to $K\subseteq L\subseteq L'$, gives the two fully typed equations
$$\begin{aligned}
\operatorname{Ex}_{e,E}
&=Rg_{L',*}(C_{a_T,b_T,E})\circ\operatorname{Ex}_{b,E_{K'}}\circ b_S^{-1}(\operatorname{Ex}_{a,E})\circ(C_{a_S,b_S,Rg_*E})^{-1},\\
\operatorname{Ex}_{e,E}
&=Rg_{L',*}(C_{r_T,c_T,E})\circ\operatorname{Ex}_{c,E_L}\circ c_S^{-1}(\operatorname{Ex}_{g,L,E})\circ(C_{r_S,c_S,Rg_*E})^{-1}.
\end{aligned}$$
Both are maps $e_S^{-1}Rg_*E\to Rg_{L',*}e_T^{-1}E$, since the outer squares have the same composite projections. All $C$ factors are isomorphisms. By Step 8 the first expression is invertible, and the factors $Rg_{L',*}(C_{r_T,c_T,E})$ and $\operatorname{Ex}_{c,E_L}$ in the second expression are invertible. Cancelling those factors and $(C_{r_S,c_S,Rg_*E})^{-1}$ shows that $c_S^{-1}(\operatorname{Ex}_{g,L,E})$ is invertible. Finally $c_S^{-1}$ is an equivalence by Step 7 and reflects isomorphisms: applying its quasi-inverse to this inverse and using the unit isomorphisms supplies an inverse to $\operatorname{Ex}_{g,L,E}$. This proves the asserted isomorphism of the specified canonical map.
